# tools/ales_stereo_segment_witness.rish — stereo_segment: the voice-activity segmenter carried into stereo with ONE SHARED FRAME GRID and TWO INDEPENDENT SEGMENTATIONS, the suite's third stereo analysis rung (ALES147).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES147.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-050741_fill-ales147-lotus-stereo-segment.md
# Stands on: lotus/segment.rye (ALES95 — the mono voice-activity segmenter run once per channel, its segment, its Segments run list, its Segment run, its SegmentError, all reused whole), lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94 — the
# per-frame classifier the segmenter sequences), lotus/schmitt.rye (ALES92 — the band's own precheck), lotus/meter.rye (ALES13 — sample_peak_max, the floor ceiling), lotus/stereo_voiced.rye (ALES146 — the stereo analysis shape
# this mirrors, one shared setting with per-channel independence), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip, two mono Clips left/right, its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip). A Lotus master is a StereoClip
# whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length. Where ALES146 carried the per-span classifier into stereo, this rung carries the reader that runs that classifier across a whole clip, frame by frame,
# coalescing neighbouring frames of the same verdict into runs: two segmenters reading two channels, each producing its own run-length label sequence, writing not one sample. Each channel is segmented on its own frames, so the
# two segmentations are independent in the count and boundaries of their runs — a voiced-then-silent left produces two runs where a uniform right stays one.
#
# lotus/stereo_segment.rye validates the shared frame length, band, span, and floor once before either channel is read (BadThreshold on an illegal band, BadRange on a span outside the samples, BadFloor on a floor above the peak
# ceiling, BadFrame on a zero/oversized/non-dividing length), then segments both channels through ALES95's segment into an out-pointer StereoSegments; SegmentError reused whole. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO SEGMENT LAW:
# each channel's runs equal ALES95's mono segment with the same setting, run for run. THE READ-ONLY / BALANCE LAW: the source StereoClip byte for byte unchanged on both, the pair balanced. THE INDEPENDENT-SEGMENTATION LAW: a
# voiced-then-silent left producing two runs where a uniform right stays one, an identical master producing identical runs, each channel's runs tiling its span with no adjacent runs sharing a verdict. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE
# LAW: a shared BadFrame / BadFloor / BadThreshold / BadRange refusing before either channel is segmented with both lists and the clip untouched, a uniform channel one run on both.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_segment_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-segment: Language EN — stereo_segment splits a span in both channels of a stereo master into consecutive frames, classifies each frame as silent, voiced, or unvoiced through one shared setting, and coalesces neighbouring frames of the same verdict into runs, writing each channel's run-length label sequence into an out-pointer and writing not one sample. A Lotus master is two Clips heard together, one per speaker, whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length. This rung carries the suite's voice-activity loop into stereo on ALES95's segmenter, running the proven mono segmenter on each channel with the same frame grid, validated once against the shared frame length, band, span, and floor before either channel is read so a refusal never segments one channel and leaves the other's run list half-written."
say "ales-stereo-segment: Style Radiant — where ALES146 carried the per-span classifier into stereo, two verdicts over one span, this rung carries the reader that runs that classifier across a whole clip: the voice-activity loop at the heart of every silence trimmer and auto-splitter, classify each frame then coalesce neighbouring frames of the same verdict into runs. It mirrors the stereo classifier and the stereo meter, and since a run list is far too large to return by value it writes both channels' runs into an out-pointer StereoSegments, the discipline splice and feed keep. The honest signature of a stereo analysis rung shows at its deepest here: each channel is segmented on its own frames, so the two segmentations are independent not merely in a scalar but in the count and boundaries of their runs, an identical-channel master producing the same runs on both while a channel that goes voiced then silent segments into two runs where a uniform channel stays one. The crux is the sequencing rule carried up one level, in stereo: ALES95's segmentation equals a decision built by hand from repeated classify calls coalesced, so each channel's segmentation equals ALES95's mono segment, and the stereo segmenter can never drift from the mono segmenter, which cannot drift from the classifier, which cannot drift from its two readers, the tower of readers staying a tower. The rule is stated positively: the segmenter reads only, the source clip leaving byte for byte unchanged on both channels, each output a run-length label sequence over one channel, not a transcript or a diarization. It reuses ALES95's SegmentError by name, ALES95's segmenter for the runs, and ALES92's precheck for the band-and-span law, inventing no new fault. The crux stands out as four laws: the stereo segment law, the read-only / balance law, the independent-segmentation law (a voiced-then-silent left producing two runs where a uniform right stays one, an identical master producing identical runs, each channel's runs tiling its span with no adjacent runs sharing a verdict), and the atomicity / degenerate law (a shared BadFrame / BadFloor / BadThreshold / BadRange refusing before either channel is segmented with both lists and the clip untouched, a uniform channel one run on both)."
say "ales-stereo-segment: Lens TAME — two bounded i16 Clips (left, right) read in lockstep by one shared frame length, band, floor, and split; the balance invariant is asserted on entry and exit, the shared band and span are bound-checked once via ALES92's precheck, the floor once against meter's sample_peak_max, and the frame-length divisibility once before any read, and because those checks hold identically for both balanced channels each mono segment is pre-validated to succeed so no refusal can land between the two channel run lists or leave one half-written; the segmenter composes ALES95's segment per channel through an out-pointer StereoSegments (two Segments, each bounded at max_segments, written never returned), which sequences ALES94's classify over consecutive frames reading its features without writing a sample; the stereo segment law is proven against ALES95's own segment run per channel across three frame lengths, three floors, and three splits, the independent-segmentation law proves a voiced-then-silent left yields two runs while a uniform right yields one and an identical master yields identical runs, coverage is proven as data (the runs tile the span with no adjacent runs sharing a verdict per channel), the read-only law proves the source clip is byte-for-byte unchanged on both, and the atomicity cases leave both run lists and the clip untouched with a uniform master one run on both; siloed to lotus/, read-only, no adaptive floor, no windowing overlap, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_segment"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_segment.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-segment: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_segment.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-segment: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-segment: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-segment' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-segment: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-segment line"
